// A reassigned fn-typed local keeps the authority of its FIRST binding when
// the reassignment sits in the arm of a statement-position `match`.
// `build_local_closure_caps_block` walked Let, Assign, DenyBlock, If, For,
// While, Select, TryCatch and a bare block, but a `match` statement is an
// `Expr`, not a `Stmt::If`, so its arm bodies were never visited: the call
// `f()` inside `deny!(fs:read)` was charged the pure `harmless`.
// ACCEPTANCE: rejected (E0507) in default and --strict-capabilities mode.
// A compile that prints the secret means the arm was not walked.

// NOT AN ESCAPE, and a deliberate over-rejection. The match is the whole body
// of a lambda bound with `let`, and a lambda captures `f` BY VALUE: calling
// `t()` never changes the outer `f`, so this program prints "not a secret"
// on master and on the branch alike (run it with the check skipped to see).
// The capability builder keeps one flat name -> authority map and does not
// know a lambda body is a separate scope, so the reassignment inside it is
// unioned into the outer `f` and `f()` inside `deny!(fs:read)` is rejected.
// Sound, and the price of a flat map; precision on this shape is lower than
// master (which accepted it). The marker below carries no word the leak
// instrument matches, so a program that did print the secret would show
// TOPSECRET and nothing else would.

fn harmless() -> str {
    return "not a secret"
}
@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

fn outer(reader: fn() -> str) {
    let mut f: fn() -> str = harmless
    let n = 1
    let t = || {
        match n { 1 => { f = reader } _ => { } }
    }
    t()
    deny!(fs:read) {
        println("OUT: " + f())
    }
}
@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    outer(reader)
}
